skill: Spring Boot

@ Dependency Injection | B
What is dependency injection?
= A pattern where an object receives the objects it depends on from outside instead of creating them itself
- A way of copying database rows between tables
- A technique for compiling Java faster
- A keyword used for inheritance
? Spring creates and wires beans, so classes depend on abstractions and are easier to test and change.

@ Dependency Injection | B
Which annotation marks a class as a Spring-managed bean that is found by component scanning?
= @Component
- @Entity
- @Table
- @Override
? @Component (and stereotypes such as @Service and @Repository) tell Spring to create and manage an instance.

@ Dependency Injection | I
Which injection style does Spring generally recommend?
= Constructor injection
- Field injection
- Injection through static fields
- Injection by reflection on private methods
? Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, allows final fields and keeps classes easy to unit test.

@ Dependency Injection | I
What does the @Autowired annotation do?
= Asks Spring to inject a matching bean as a dependency
- Creates the database tables
- Maps a method to a URL
- Secures an endpoint
? Spring looks up a bean of the required type in the application context and supplies it.

@ Dependency Injection | I
What is the default scope of a Spring bean?
= Singleton
- Prototype
- Request
- Session
? By default Spring creates one shared instance of each bean per application context.

@ Spring Boot Basics | B
Which annotation combines @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration and @ComponentScan?
= @SpringBootApplication
- @EnableWebMvc
- @SpringBootTest
- @ComponentApplication
? @SpringBootApplication is placed on the main class and enables auto-configuration and scanning.

@ Spring Boot Basics | B
What is the purpose of a Spring Boot starter dependency?
= It brings in a curated set of libraries for a feature, such as web or JPA
- It starts the application server in the background
- It generates the database
- It encrypts the application properties
? Starters such as spring-boot-starter-web reduce manual dependency management.

@ Spring Boot Basics | I
Which annotation turns a class into a controller whose methods return JSON responses?
= @RestController
- @Service
- @Repository
- @Configuration
? @RestController combines @Controller and @ResponseBody so return values are written to the response body.

@ Spring Boot Basics | I
Which annotation binds the variable in a URL such as /users/{id} to a method parameter?
= @PathVariable
- @RequestParam
- @RequestBody
- @ModelAttribute
? @PathVariable reads a value from the URI template. @RequestParam reads query parameters.

@ Spring Boot Basics | I
Which annotation reads the JSON in a request body into a Java object?
= @RequestBody
- @PathVariable
- @Autowired
- @Qualifier
? @RequestBody uses an HttpMessageConverter (Jackson) to deserialize the request body.

@ Spring Boot Basics | I
What does Spring Boot Actuator provide?
= Production-ready endpoints such as health and metrics
- A graphical user interface framework
- A database migration tool
- An object-relational mapper
? Actuator exposes operational information, for example /actuator/health.

@ Configuration | I
Where does Spring Boot read settings such as server.port by default?
= application.properties or application.yml
- pom.xml only
- web.xml only
- The main class only
? External configuration files are loaded from the classpath and can be overridden by environment variables.

@ Configuration | I
What does @Profile("dev") on a bean definition do?
= Registers the bean only when the dev profile is active
- Gives the bean a fixed name
- Makes the bean a prototype
- Disables the bean in all environments
? Profiles let you provide different beans or settings for environments such as dev and prod.

@ Spring Data JPA | I
Which interface do you extend to get ready-made CRUD methods for an entity?
= JpaRepository
- EntityManager
- DataSource
- RestTemplate
? Extending JpaRepository gives methods such as save, findById and findAll without writing implementations.

@ Spring Data JPA | I
What does a repository method named findByEmail(String email) do in Spring Data JPA?
= Spring derives a query that finds entities by the email field
- It must always be annotated with @Query
- It is not allowed
- It returns every row regardless of the email
? Spring Data parses the method name and generates the query automatically.

@ Spring Data JPA | A
What does @Transactional do on a service method?
= Runs the method in a transaction that commits on success and rolls back on a runtime exception by default
- Makes the method run asynchronously
- Caches the result in memory
- Secures the method with a password
? Spring opens a transaction before the method and commits or rolls it back when it finishes.

@ Spring Security | I
What does BCryptPasswordEncoder do?
= Hashes passwords using a salted, adaptive algorithm
- Encrypts passwords so they can be decrypted later
- Generates JWT tokens
- Stores user sessions
? Passwords should be hashed, not encrypted. BCrypt adds a salt and is intentionally slow to resist brute force.

@ Testing | I
Which annotation starts the full Spring application context for an integration test?
= @SpringBootTest
- @Test
- @Mock
- @Autowired
? @SpringBootTest loads the complete context. @Test only marks a test method.
